---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SeoToolExtras from "../../../components/SeoToolExtras.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Why convert SRT to plain text for translation?",
    answer:
      "Translators often work in CAT tools or Word without timing codes. Plain text avoids accidental edits to timestamps and reduces file format confusion.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I include timestamps in the export?",
    answer:
      "For translation-only handoff, strip timestamps. Keep timestamps if the reviewer must match lines to video timecodes.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is SRT to text the same as transcription?",
    answer:
      "No. You already have subtitles; you are extracting dialogue. Transcription creates text from audio.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the converter upload my file?",
    answer:
      "Subtitle Kit runs in the browser. Your SRT is not sent to our servers for conversion.",
  },
  {
    question: "What about speaker labels?",
    answer:
      "Standard SRT rarely includes speakers. If your file has custom formatting, clean it in the editor before export.",
  },
];

const steps = [
  "Paste SRT or open a .srt file in the browser tool.",
  "Choose plain text with or without timecodes.",
  "Copy or download for your translator or TMS.",
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SRT to text for translation — export dialogue without timing"
  description="Prepare subtitle files for translators: convert SRT to plain text in the browser, optional timestamps, no upload."
  canonicalPath="/guides/srt-to-text-for-translation/"
>
  <p class="badge">Guide · ~7 min read</p>
  <h1>SRT to text for translation workflows</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Localization teams rarely want raw SubRip with arrows and milliseconds in the first
    pass. This guide covers when to strip timecodes, how to export dialogue safely, and
    how to merge translated text back into timed captions.
  </p>

  <div class="prose-block">
    <h2>When to export SRT as plain text</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>First-pass translation in Word, Google Docs, or a TMS</li>
      <li>Client review of dialogue only (no burn-in timing)</li>
      <li>Glossary building from existing finished subs</li>
      <li>Script comparison against original language SRT</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Recommended handoff format</h2>
    <p>
      Export <strong>one line per cue</strong>, no index numbers, no timing arrows.
      Use our <a href="/tools/srt-to-text/">SRT to text tool</a> to paste or open a file locally.
      After translation, re-time in your NLE or regenerate SRT from your TMS—do not assume
      line count stays identical if target language expands.
    </p>

    <h2>Quality checklist before you send</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Remove HTML tags or ASS-style overrides if present in source SRT.</li>
      <li>Merge duplicate consecutive lines from auto-captions.</li>
      <li>Note max reading speed (chars/sec) for the target platform.</li>
      <li>Share video reference or timecoded review link separately.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>After translation returns</h2>
    <p>
      Import timed SRT from your CAT tool, or shift timing with
      <a href="/tools/srt-time-shift/">SRT time shift</a> if the new dub track differs.
      Check <a href="/tools/line-length/">caption line length</a> before publishing vertical cuts.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy for client scripts</h2>
    <p>
      Unreleased series and legal review scripts should not go to random «upload your SRT»
      converters. Browser-local conversion keeps content on the freelancer’s device—document
      that in your SOW.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="cta-row">
    <a class="btn" href="/tools/srt-to-text/">Convert SRT to text</a>
  </p>

  <SeoToolExtras
    faqs={faqs}
    toolName="SRT to text for translation"
    toolDescription="Browser tool to export subtitle dialogue as plain text for translators."
    path="/guides/srt-to-text-for-translation/"
    steps={steps}
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cta-row { margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem; }
</style>
